        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"partial\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n  \"useful_material\": \"The supplied text is a review article that summarizes current knowledge on microbiota-derived metabolites in cancer immunotherapy. It identifies specific metabolites of interest for the gap, including inosine, short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs like butyrate and valerate), tryptophan-derived indoles, and TMAO. Crucially for the gap question, the authors state that while microbial molecules are detected in human plasma (estimated 5-10% of plasma metabolites), the mechanisms of direct interaction within the tumor microenvironment (TME) are 'still poorly understood.' The text highlights that key findings regarding metabolite efficacy (e.g., inosine enhancing ICI, SCFAs enhancing CTLs) are derived from 'experimental tumor models' (mice) or 'in vitro killing assays,' rather than direct intratumoral quantification in humans. It also notes that intratumoral bacteria have been characterized via 16S rRNA sequencing in human tumors, but does not describe corresponding metabolite quantification in those human samples.\",\n  \"auxiliary_material\": \"The review provides mechanistic context for why intratumoral quantification is relevant, detailing pathways such as SCFAs acting as HDAC inhibitors or inosine modulating T cells via adenosine A2A receptors. It mentions challenges in delivery and measurement, noting that oral administration of metabolites is moderately efficacious and that novel delivery strategies are needed. It also distinguishes between gut microbiota and the 'oncobiome' (intratumoral bacteria), suggesting that local microbial signatures vary by cancer type (e.g., Fusobacterium in CRC, Proteobacteria in PDAC), which implies that intratumoral metabolite profiles might also vary.\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"The supplied material does not identify a specific study that successfully performed direct mass spectrometry-based quantification of microbial metabolites within human tumor tissue from ICI-treated patients. While it confirms that plasma metabolites are measured and intratumoral bacteria are sequenced, it does not explicitly state that intratumoral metabolite quantification in humans is absent, though it strongly implies this by attributing mechanistic metabolite data to preclinical models. The success criterion regarding 'confirmation that this remains a methodological gap' is supported but not definitively closed by this review's silence on human intratumoral metabolomics.\",\n  \"outline_action\": \"Use this review to list the candidate metabolites (inosine, SCFAs) and the current reliance on mouse models for mechanistic validation. Cite the authors' statement that direct TME interaction mechanisms are poorly understood to support the argument that human intratumoral metabolite data is lacking. To fulfill the gap completely, search for primary research articles cited in this review (e.g., Mager et al., 2020; Luu et al., 2021) to verify if they included any human intratumoral metabolomics, and conduct a targeted search for 'intratumoral metabolomics' or 'tumor tissue mass spectrometry' combined with 'immune checkpoint inhibitor' to find any studies not covered here.\"\n}",
